gameplay-wise quantum break has a lot in  common with previous remedy games it  takes fairly unremarkable third-person  shooting and makes it exciting with a  gimmick Max Payne had cinematic bullet  time and Alan Wake had weaponized light  in quantum break it's time based  superpowers there's at least four of  them in fact and when layered together  they make combat really intense and  strategic let's start with the core  shooting it's functional but to be  honest it lacks the feedback you got  when pumping bullets into gears Awards  locusts or binary domains robots  shooting rifles and SM Jesus and  particularly exhilarating by its  effective in that death by a thousand  cuts kind of way grab a shotgun and get  close though and you're working with  Mike Tyson grade uppercuts for that  desperate situation when you run out of  ammo on primary weapons you've got a  very handy pistol with a bottomless clip  weapons in hand it's simply a case of  bouncing around cover and gunning down  everything in your part now that may  sound pretty uninspired and yeah it kind  of is but it's also a solid foundation  on which remedy build something far more  exciting let's backtrack a bit before we  get into that  catchy  bonham break opens with a classic time  travel experiment gone wrong in the  aftermath Ice Man aka Jack Joyce is  being hunted by a mega evil organization  called monarch the upshot for Joyce is  he acquires the ability to manipulate  zero state energy which is essentially  science talk for almost frozen time  think hero from heroes but without the  yotta every five minutes  it's this time bending superpower that  elevates combat the first power time  vision acts like Batman's detective mode  and highlights all threads in red since  different enemy types have distinct  silhouettes this was a handy way of  giving everyone an ocular pat-down with  the time stop ability you can fire a  bubble of frozen time to bring things to  a grinding halt for a few seconds if  you're quick you can freeze a grenade  coming your way and scoot out of the  blast zone or trap an enemy next to an  explosive barrel and well you know what  happens next it's a versatile tool that  has defensive offensive and strategic  applications so it became a bit of a  crutch the third power time dodge is  essentially a blink and if you're  familiar with dragon ball z you'll know  there are few things as awesome as an  instant teleport so when you vanish  right before charging enemy's eyes and  reappear behind them to empty a clip  into his back you can't help but smile  the last power lets you become neo from  the matrix for a few seconds and nullify  all incoming damage time shield was  really useful when we let the power trip  get to our heads and got overwhelmed  which as it turns out was all the time  with all that power you think it'd be  easy to steamroll through everyone but  there are nuances to the way powers work  to stop that from happening since each  ability has a cooldown period you need  to be mindful of recovery times while  time vision can be spammed time stop  time dodge and time shield need to be  micromanage for effective use and trust  me you'll need to start getting really  effective with your powers really fast  your enemies are remarkably aggressive  and pretty intelligent too when in cover  their shower you have bullets to execute  flanks and if you have a tendency of  hiding behind objects they'll use  grenades to flush you out into the open  at times it converge on overwhelming but  that pressure is by design it's remedy  turning otherwise overpowered skills  into tools for survival skirmishes are  painfully intense flirting between power  trips and desperate crisis management  the constant de fleeting and recharging  of abilities gives combat and  electrifying ebb and flow enemies react  to your actions and call out to each  other when you use powers and then start  compensating for them as it turns out  you can also manipulate their teamwork  by concentrating gunfire or you  abilities to follow them into choke  points but those glorious moments when  you're firing on all cylinders the  synergy between abilities gives you a  control over the battlefield that  borders on predatory there's an  explosive energy to moving around the  environment that makes you feel super  heroic and that feeling intensifies as  you upgrade abilities and make them more  powerful there were a couple of niggles  during our hands-on aiming always felt a  bit off regardless of the sensitivity  setting and the act of moving from cover  to cover wasn't particularly satisfying  we could always teleport between points  and move around the environment that way  I always felt like stepping away from  drama but over all this stuff wasn't a  deal-breaker what do you want of course  remedy is also trying some ambitious  things with storytelling in quantum  break specifically how it's delivering  it while the game focuses on Jack  Joyce's side of the story 30-minute  live-action episodes between each act  follow bad guy Paul serene there are  junctions where player choices impact  both stories and neat touches where  live-action focuses on characters that  had otherwise be forgettable but whether  all this will be enough to successfully  unify video games and live action is the  question time will tell but at the very  least it looks like the studio is  anchoring its ambitions on shooting that  feels empowering and thrilling for more  on quantum break check out gamespot com
 

